# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.DS_Store

# Build artefacts. Anchored to the repo root on purpose: an unanchored
# `build/` also matches `scripts/build/`, which is where the catalog generator
# lives -- so the script that writes `config/prg.yaml` was invisible to git
# while the file it writes was tracked, and the gate that runs it would have
# failed on a fresh clone with "no such file". A pattern meant for build output
# must not swallow source.
/dist/
/build/
/target/
# Built by `maturin develop`; the wheel is built from source, so the
# extension is a build artefact like `target/` and `dist/` above.
src/alea/_core/*.so

# Scratch
tmp/

# Snakemake's own state. It holds the scheduler's cache, locks and metadata for
# this machine's runs, not anything a clean clone reproduces from.
.snakemake/

# DVC. The cache is local and rebuildable; the control files (`.dvc/`,
# `dvc.yaml`, `dvc.lock`) are what a clean clone reproduces from and are
# committed.
.dvc/cache/
.dvc/tmp/

# The data lake, narrowed.
#
# This section previously read `data/`, which ignored the manual exports the
# stages read: `_require_raw` resolves a `Access.MANUAL` source to
# `data/raw/<source_id>/manual/<file>`, and `02-data-pipeline.md` §1.1 says the
# pipeline reads "the committed export" -- a committed file that a wholesale
# `data/` ignore makes uncommittable. The stage then raises with a message
# telling the operator to commit a file git will not accept.
#
# What is ignored is the fetched layer and the DVC-managed lake bodies, not the
# lake. `data/raw/<source>/<checksum>/` is content-addressed and rebuildable;
# `data/raw/<source>/manual/` is a reviewed human export and is tracked.
data/raw/*/*/
!data/raw/*/manual/
# The fetch marker `rules/fetch.smk` writes: the scheduler's own bookkeeping for
# this machine, not data.
data/raw/*/.fetched
data/processed/
data/interim/

# `results/` was ignored wholesale, which contradicts `01-foundations.md` §6:
# "`resources/`, `results/` -- tracked where a result is load-bearing (a
# published capacity mix), ignored where it is scratch." A DVC pointer file
# (`.dvc`) is load-bearing: it is what a clean clone checks out the body from.
results/*
!results/*.dvc

# `results/runs.jsonl` is deliberately *not* excepted, and the first draft of
# this file excepted it. `alea.api.runs.RunLog` reads it, and the CLI's `_Run`
# appends a line on *every* invocation -- including a `config validate` that
# only prints. Tracking an append-only file that every command writes makes
# `git status` dirty after every command, which is how a dirty check gets
# widened until it is bypassed. The reader treats a missing file as "no runs",
# so nothing requires it to be committed; it is runtime state that belongs to
# the machine that produced it, like the fetch marker above.

# The negation above is defeated if a later rule ignores a *parent* directory --
# git does not descend into an excluded directory, so no `!` inside it can
# re-include a file. Nothing here excludes `results/` itself; this comment is
# the warning for whoever adds one.

# The extraction cache. Content-addressed by prompt version, model, temperature
# and the text itself (`alea.scanning.extract.llm.LlmCache.key`), so an entry is
# reproducible from the call that made it and its whole purpose is to avoid
# repeating a provider call. It is per-machine, like `.dvc/cache/` above, and
# a committed cache would ship one operator's responses to every clone.
resources/scanning/llm_cache/

# Generated outputs. A report is rebuilt from the artefacts it cites, and a
# model file is a build product of the solve that produced it.
reports/*/
models/

# Deliberately NOT ignored, and both were:
#
#   `*.nc` / `*.nc.aux` -- `02-data-pipeline.md` §1.1 requires a small Kenya
#   cutout committed as the CI fixture, and a blanket `*.nc` ignore excludes
#   exactly that file. The large cutouts live under `data/`, which is ignored
#   above; a NetCDF elsewhere in the tree is there on purpose.
#
#   `Cargo.lock` -- this workspace holds no binaries (every crate is a library
#   or the `cdylib` behind `alea._core._rust`), so Cargo's guidance makes the
#   lockfile optional. It is tracked anyway: the crate graph is compiled into a
#   wheel that ships, `rust-toolchain.toml` and `uv.lock` already pin the other
#   two halves of the toolchain, and a lockfile is the only thing that keeps two
#   builds of one commit from resolving different crate versions.

# The workspace search index. A local tool's cache of the tree it indexed --
# 33 MB of vectors, rebuilt from source on demand, and stale the moment a file
# changes. Committing it would put a binary blob in every clone and in every
# diff that touches it, for no reader's benefit.
.zvec-grep/

# Timestamped backups written by the ops-safety rule before editing shared
# config. They are the previous state of a file that is already in the tree;
# committing one puts a whole duplicate of `config/prg.yaml` in history.
*.bak.[0-9]*-[0-9]*
